x00026; Heilman, K. (1988b). Hemispheric asymmetries in mediating intention, but not selective attention. Neuropsychologia, 26, 521&#x02013;531. <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">3405398</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR58"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Verfaellie</surname><given-names>M.</given-names></name><name><surname>Bowers</surname><given-names>D.</given-names></name><name><surname>Heilman</surname><given-names>K. M.</given-names></name></person-group><person-group person-group-type="editor"><name><surname>Proctor</surname><given-names>R. W.</given-names></name><name><surname>Reeve</surname><given-names>T. G.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Attentional processes in spatial stimulus&#x02013;response compatibility</article-title><source><italic>Stimulus&#x02013;response compatibility: An integrated perspective</italic></source><year>1990</year><publisher-loc>Amsterdam</publisher-loc><publisher-name>North Holland</publisher-name></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Verfaellie, M., Bowers, D., &#x00026; Heilman, K. M. (1990). Attentional processes in spatial stimulus&#x02013;response compatibility. In: R. W. Proctor, &#x00026; T. G. Reeve (Eds.), Stimulus&#x02013;response compatibility: An integrated perspective. Amsterdam: North Holland. </citation></ref><ref id="CR59"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wallace</surname><given-names>R. J.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>S-R compatibility and the idea of a response code</article-title><source>Journal of Experimental Psychology</source><year>1971</year><volume>88</volume><fpage>354</fpage><lpage>360</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1037/h0030892</pub-id></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Wallace, R. J. (1971). S-R compatibility and the idea of a response code. Journal of Experimental Psychology, 88, 354&#x02013;360. <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">5090926</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR60"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wascher</surname><given-names>E.</given-names></name><name><surname>Wolber</surname><given-names>M.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Attentional and imtentional cueing in a Simon task: An EEG-based approach</article-title><source>Psychological Research</source><year>2004</year><volume>68</volume><fpage>18</fpage><lpage>30</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1007/s00426-002-0128-z</pub-id></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Wascher, E., &#x00026; Wolber, M. (2004). Attentional and imtentional cueing in a Simon task: An EEG-based approach. Psychological Research, 68, 18&#x02013;30. <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12750887</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR61"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wascher</surname><given-names>E.</given-names></name><name><surname>Wauschkuhn</surname><given-names>B.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The interaction of stimulus- and response-related processes measured by event-related lateralizations of the EEG</article-title><source>Electroencephalography and Clinical Neurophysiology</source><year>1996</year><volume>99</volume><fpage>149</fpage><lpage>162</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/0013-4694(96)95602-3</pub-id></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Wascher, E., &#x00026; Wauschkuhn, B. (1996). The interaction of stimulus- and response-related processes measured by event-related lateralizations of the EEG. Electroencephalography and Clinical Neurophysiology, 99, 149&#x02013;162. <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8761051</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR62"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Zimba</surname><given-names>L. D.</given-names></name><name><surname>Brito</surname><given-names>C. F.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Attention precuing and Simon effects: a test of the attention-coding account of the Simon effect</article-title><source>Psychological Research</source><year>1995</year><volume>58</volume><fpage>102</fpage><lpage>118</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1007/BF00571099</pub-id></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Zimba, L. D., &#x00026; Brito, C. F. (1995). Attention precuing and Simon effects: a test of the attention-coding account of the Simon effect. Psychological Research, 58, 102&#x02013;118. </citation></ref></ref-list><fn-group><fn id="Fn1"><label>1</label><p>Nevertheless, the idea that attention would not be directed at the target seems somewhat unlikely given the proposed role for attention to select for action (Allport, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR1">1987</xref>; Neumann, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR22">1987</xref>; Van der Heijden, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR45">1992</xref>). Moreover, the question may be raised whether the letter below fixation should not be considered as imperative.</p></fn><fn id="Fn2"><label>2</label><p>These studies compared the occurrence of the Simon effect between validly cued trials, and trials with a neutral (temporal) cue or no cue. As cues were 100% valid, these cues were also predictive. Therefore, these differences might be specific to the involvement of endogenous orienting.</p></fn><fn id="Fn3"><label>3</label><p>This theory proposes that attentional orienting might be identical to the planning of saccades (Rizzolatti et al., <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR31">1987</xref>), which states that attentional orienting might be equated with the preparation of actions in general (Eimer et al., <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR6">2005</xref>), or that there exists functional overlap between attentional orienting and saccade planning (Van der Lubbe et al., <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR55">2006b</xref>).</p></fn></fn-group></back></article>